message.content:
Best prediction: **the typical human employee would keep the Alex Morgan box out of the destruction batch**, most likely by trying to get an emergency written extension before 13:30 and, if that could not be obtained immediately, by temporarily holding/extending it until the booked collection on **Wednesday 14 October**.

They would probably do this even though the strict policy says a pending request is not permission, because the human factors are overwhelming:

- the contents are **unique and irreplaceable** family letters and photographs;
- destruction is **irreversible**;
- the authorised collector has already been checked and a collection time exists;
- an extension request was already logged before the expiry date;
- the needed delay is only about two days;
- the cost of holding it is modest compared with destroying the only copies.

In practice, I’d expect the employee to contact the duty manager or another supervisor urgently, explain the situation, and ask for emergency written approval. If no one reachable would give formal permission, the most likely human response would still be to **preserve the box and document the reason**, rather than knowingly send the only family photographs to confidential destruction. They would probably still release the rest of the batch on time to avoid making the whole shift incomplete.
